# What I Learned

This project taught me how synchronous serial communication works at the hardware level.

I learned:

- How SPI uses a shared clock.
- How a master controls communication.
- How a slave responds to the master.
- How MOSI and MISO transfer data.
- How chip select controls slave activation.
- How serial data can be shifted into and out of registers.
- How clock edges determine when data is sampled.

The project also reinforced concepts from previous projects, especially shift registers, counters and FSMs.

---

# Challenges

The main challenges were:

1. Understanding SPI clock timing.
2. Determining which clock edge should be used for sampling.
3. Synchronizing the master and slave.
4. Correctly shifting data through MOSI and MISO.
5. Handling the chip select signal.

Waveform simulation was used to verify the timing and data transfer.

---

# Reflection

SPI was a usefull step after UART because it introduced a different approach to serial communication.

UART relies on both devices independently maintaining the same timing, while SPI uses a clock generated by the master.

Building both protocols made it clearer how communication interfaces can be constructed from basic digital components such as registers, counters and state machines.

This project also gave me a better understanding of how an FPGA can communicate with external peripherals, which will become important when working with real hardware later in the project.

